$\Delta G^{\circ}$ is related to thermodynamic equilibrium constant as $\Delta \mathrm{G}^{\circ}=-2303 \mathrm{RT} \log \mathrm{K}\begin{aligned}
& =-2303 \times 8.314 \times 298 \log \left(26 \times 10^8\right) \\
& =-48014.50 \mathrm{~J}=-48.14 \mathrm{~kJ}
\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{C}+\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{O}_2 \longrightarrow \mathrm{CO}, \Delta H=-110 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \ldots \text { (i) } \\
& \mathrm{C}+\mathrm{O}_2 \longrightarrow \mathrm{CO}_2, \quad \Delta H=-393 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\
& \ldots \text { (ii) }
\end{aligned}$
On subtracting Eq. (i) from Eq. (ii), we get
$\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{CO} & +\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{O}_2 \longrightarrow \mathrm{CO}_2 \\
\Delta H & =393-(-110) \\
& =-283 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}
\end{aligned}$

In the reaction, two moles of $\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{H}$ bonds and two moles of $\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{Cl}$ bonds are broken.
Hence, energy absorbed
$\begin{aligned}
& =2 \times 416+2 \times 325 \\
& =1482 \mathrm{~kJ}
\end{aligned}$
